# JavaScript and TypeScript Standards

- Use strict typing and avoid `any`; prefer explicit interfaces and utility types.
- Keep exported APIs stable and strongly typed across app and library boundaries.
- Write JSDoc for all exported functions and classes, including params, returns, and thrown errors.
- Prefer pure functions and dependency injection over hidden shared mutable state.
- Handle async errors explicitly; avoid empty `catch` blocks and swallowed promise rejections.
- Favor clear, readable control flow over clever one-liners.
- Reuse existing helpers before creating new utility abstractions.
- Keep modules cohesive: one domain responsibility per file where practical.
